	Documentation for the F editor for Unix, "nf".
			     By
			Stephen Willson
		  Halloween, October 31, 1981

		Last update:  September 15, 1982




*** **  *** F for Unix: nf: a "tutorial introduction".  *** ** ***

This editor is based on the editor by Stephen Willson and Eric Olsen
for the UCSD Pascal system, which in turn is based on the editor by
James R. Meehan for the Dec-10 and Dec-20 systems at UCI.  These editors
are written in C, UCSD Pascal, IMP10, and IMP20 respectively.  The command
structure is basically the same for all of these editors, however, the
editors by Willson and Olsen, and by Willson keep the entire file in
memory, whereas the editors by Meehan do not.

The Vax/Unix system is supportive of an editor that keeps the entire file
in memory, as the Vax supports a huge virtual address space.  Unix also
can read and write the file to and from memory with great speed, and so
i/o overhead is minimal.

Warning:  you must set the environment variable "term" to the correct
value before using nf.  See the note toward the end of this documentation.

There are two editing buffers, called the "editing" buffer and the "put"
buffer.  The editing buffer holds the current image of the file.  The
put buffer stores deleted or copied text.  Text copied from the put
buffer to the editing buffer is INSERTED into the editing buffer.
Text copied from the editing buffer to the put buffer REPLACES text in
the put buffer.  Presently, the editing buffer is 100,000 bytes in size
and the put buffer is 50,000 bytes in size.

The basic design of the editor supports the "what you see is what you
get" philosophy of editing.  The editor behaves as if the terminal
screen is a piece of glass 80 columns by 23 lines which can be moved
about over the text of the file as if that text were printed out on a
sheet of paper.  This piece of glass, or window, as it is known, can be
moved about to show different parts of the file.  When an editing
session is started, the "window" is placed over the top left part of
the imaginary piece of paper that represents the text in the file.

The following pictures may prove instructive:

       |-----------------|
       |This is a test of|the emergency broadcasting system.
       |Now is the time f|r all good men to come to the aid
       |of their party.  |he quick brown fox jumped.
       |-----------------|

			 |----------------|
	This is a test of|the emergency br|adcasting system.
	Now is the time f|r all good men t| come to the aid
	of their party.  |he quick brown f|x jumped.
			 |----------------|


       |-----------------|the emergency broadcasting system.
       |Now is the time f|r all good men to come to the aid
       |of their party.  |he quick brown fox jumped.
       |                 |
       |-----------------|

If we had the above text in the editing buffer, but only a 20 column
by 3 line window, then the window would start out over the text as
show in the first picture.  The second picture shows the window
slid to the right (which makes the text appear to move to the left),
and the last picture shows the window moved down one line.

The editor is invoked with the following incantation:

	% nf <optional options> <optional filename>

Of course, the percent sign is printed by the shell.
	    
The <optional options> are described toward the end of this document.

This will cause the editor to read in the file (if given and if it exists),
and to position the window at the start of the file.  If the file doesn't
exist, then it is assumed that you want to create a new file, and
the editor positions the window at the beginning of an empty editing
buffer.  If the file is not given at all, the editor assumes you will
make up a name later (using the "rename" command), and allows you to
enter text as if you had given a non-existant filename.

At this point, any command can be given.  Just briefly, you will want to
know the following commands:

	<exit> control-z        Leave the editor, saving the file
				if it was modified.

	<interrupt> control-c   Asks if you really want to quit, and
				if you do, leaves the editor without updating
				your file.

	<redraw> control-u      Redraw the screen; used in case some 
				communication problem messes the picture
				up.

	<move> arrows           The cursor arrows located on the keyboard
				move the
				cursor around on the screen.  This is the
				primary method of moving the attention of
				the editor around on the current windowful
				of text.  The next editor command will take
				place (if appropriate) at the spot in the
				file indicated by the flashing cursor.

	<change> printing       Typing any printing character will replace
		 characters     the character currently under the flashing
				cursor with the new character, and move
				the cursor one place to the right.  The cursor
				will move to the next line if the replaced
				character is in column 80.

Using just these commands, you can read in a file and alter the first page
of text simply by moving the cursor to the position on the screen where
you want to make a change, and then typing a character that will REPLACE
the existing characters.  You can abort your editing session, or you can
save your changes.

You will soon find the need to move the text window, as you add more and
more text.  This is done with the following commands:

	<gotoend> control-p     Move the window so that the end of the file
				will be near the top of the window.

	<gotostart> control-o   Move the window so that it is positioned over
				the beginning of the file.  This function
				also performs some house-keeping functions to
				ensure that the internal representation of
				the file matches the position being displayed
				on the screen.

	<pluspage> control-l	Pressing the clear key will move the window
				one page further into the file.

	<minuspage> control-q   Moves the window one page toward the beginning
				of the file.

	<pluslines> control-t   Moves the window 7 lines further into the file.

	<minuslines> control-w  Moves the window 7 lines closer to the beginning
				of the file.

	    
In short order, you will find that simply replacing text is insufficient
for your editing needs.  You will then want to know the following commands:

	<insertchar> control-a  Inserts one space before the character
				under the flashing cursor.

	<deletechar> control-s  Removes the character under the flashing
				cursor.

	<insertline> control-d  Inserts a carriage return before the character
				under the flashing cursor.

	<deleteline> control-f  Will delete characters up to and including
				the next carriage return.


Text deleted by <deleteline> only will be placed in the put buffer.
The contents of this buffer can be manipulated in the following ways:

	<put> control-g         Inserts the characters from the put buffer
				before the character under the flashing
				cursor.
	    
	<pick> control-k        Will copy characters from and including
				the character under the flashing cursor up to
				and including the next carriage return into
				the put buffer.

It is possible to search for text using these two commands:

	<+search> control-r     Will place the cursor at the beginning
				of the text that matches the search argument
				(see below) by skipping the character under
				the flashing cursor and searching forward
				in the file.  Character case is ignored.

	<-search> control-e     Does the same thing, but searches backwards.
				There is a bug in this command.  It will
				place the cursor at the beginning of the
				line in which the search argument was found,
				instead of at the beginning of the text.
				This will be fixed when and if get I
				around to it.

The argument to either command is entered by pressing the escape key followed
by the text to be searched for followed by the command key control-e or 
control-r.  
	Note: on some terminal types, the escape key is entered by pressing
	a different key.  On z19's, this key is "f1".  On colorscan-10's, 
	this key is "pf4".
If no argument is given, the last argument to either command
will be used.  If no argument has been given, an error will result, and your
entire file will be deleted.  (Just kidding.)

There are three other commands that can be used to move the cursor around 
on the screen, and you will soon want to use these, which are the following:

	<eol> control-v         Move the cursor to the end of the line or
				the right edge of the window, 
				whichever comes first.

	<tab> tab               Move the cursor to the next tabstop or the
	control-i               end of the window, whichever comes first.
				Tabs stops are every eight characters.

	<home>                  Pressing the home key will place the cursor
	control-y               in the top left corner of the window screen.

To switch to another file, use the <setfile> command.

	<setfile> control-b     Switch to last file.  If you give this
				an argument, then it will set to the
				file you name.

Sometimes (for instance, if you try to edit a write protected file), you
will find that the editor won't let you save your editing changes.  This
can be overcome using the command described below:

	<rename> control-x/     Changes the file name that the editor
		 control-b	will use to save your file.  This can
				also be used to set the name if no name
				was given at the start of the editing 
				session. (Note: control-x/control-b
				means to hit control-x and then
				control-b and NOT to hit control-x OR
				control-b.)

If you want to write your changes on a different file, use the <rename>
command before using the <exit> command.

You will soon find it tedious to only be able to insert or delete one
character or one line.  Actually, you can give any of these commands
(and <put> and <pick>) an argument, which is done as follows:

	Press the escape key.  (This echoes as an asterisk "*".)
	(See the Note above about the escape key on different terminals.)

	Do one and only one of the following:

		Move the cursor keys, or
		Type a number, or
		Type some text (printing characters only)

	Then type the key that activates the command you want to have
	this argument apply to.  The command will be executed using
	the argument (if it makes sense), and then the argument will
	be forgotten.  (Note how this is different from other versions
	of F: they remember arguments and make them the new default
	values.)

Doing one of the three things mentioned above will have the following effects:

	Moving the cursor using the arrow and home keys:

		For insert/delete spaces: Only the left and right keys
			may be used, and will indicate the number of spaces
			to insert.  Simply typing <escape> means to enter one
			space.  Escape followed by three right arrows will
			insert four spaces.  (The escape counts as one space.)

		For insert/delete lines: The vertical displacement downward
			in the file indicates the number of carriage returns
			to be inserted or deleted.  The horizontal displacement
			from the left margin indicates how many spaces should
			be inserted or deleted after the lines have been
			changed.  An exception:  if no vertical displacement
			is indicated, then the horizontal displacement is taken
			from the original cursor position, as for insert/
			delete spaces above.

		For pick: This will cause <pick> to pick up the text indicated
			using the same scheme as for insert/delete lines above.


	Entering a number:

		For all the functions above, indicates the number of
		characters or lines to be inserted or deleted or picked
		up as appropriate for each command.  For <pick> this refers
		to the number of lines to be picked up.

		For <pluspage> and <minuspage> this reflects the number
		of pages to be skipped, and for <plusline> and <minusline>
		this reflects the number of lines to move the window.


	Entering some text:

		For put: This will place the text into the put buffer
		REPLACING whatever text existed in the put buffer, and
		then will <put> the text (without a carriage return)
		into the editing buffer before the character under the
		flashing cursor.  This is a handy way to insert text.

		As mentioned above, typing <escape>text and then one
		of the search commands or <setfile> will change the
		parameter of that command as appropriate.  (The argument
		to the search commands is not forgotten.  This is an
		exception to the rule.)

If you decide you want to leave parameter mode, as it is called after you
have typed an escape, then you may type control-c to return to the normal
editing mode.  If you should type two control-c's by accident, then you will
be prompted to see if you really want to leave the editor.


*** <optional options> (these are not case sensitive)

-txxx	set terminal type to xxx
-hnn	set terminal height nn
-rnn	put cursor at row nn initially
-cnn	put cursor at column nn initially
-emess	display mess on the bottom line of the screen at startup
	(should be enclosed in quotes thus: "-eToo many right paren's")


*** The editor has the following known bugs:

	<-Search> puts the cursor at the beginning of the line instead of
	at the desired text.  In fact, <-Search> is a bit flaky all around.

	Makefiles get blown away when edited by nf.  This seems to have
	something to do with the fact that tabs are removed.  Very strange.
	You can fix up a destroyed Makefile with the program "unexpand."

	Tabs are removed and replaced with the appropriate number of spaces.
	You can have extra spaces converted to tabs by using the program
	"unexpand."

	If the editor can not read the file specified in the command line
	then it acts as if the file does not exist, even if it is simply
	read protected.  You may find you'll need to use <rename>
	to make it possible to save any changes you've made in another file.

Other things:

	The escape key can be used repeatedly to enter an argument.  Typing
	three escapes is the same as typing one escape and two right arrows.

	The erase eol key (on Datamedias) prints some uninteresting
	information about the editing buffers.

	The rubout key is treated the same as the backspace key, which is
	identical to the left arrow key.  The linefeed key is the same as
	down arrow.  Carriage return can be used in arguments to mean 
	"linefeed and move to start of line", as you might expect.

	Moving the cursor arrows to the edge of the window will cause them
	to stop at the edge of the window, except for the down arrow which 
	automatically scrolls the screen 7 lines.

	The editor is called "nf" instead of "f" because "f" is an
	abbreviation for the "finger" command.  "nf" stands for 
	"intuitive feeling", which is a personality type in the scheme of
	Keirsey and Bates.

	If you are fortunate enough to be using a heathkit terminal (a Z19)
	then you can use the special functions keys on the right side of the
	terminal to speed things up.  "ic" will insert a character using
	the smarts of the terminal.  "dc" deletes a character.  "il" inserts
	a line.  "dl" deletes a line.  

Choosing a terminal type:

	Nf supports the following types of terminals:
		Terminal	Name
		--------	----
		dm1520		dm1520
		heath/z19	heath
		colorscan-10	vt100

For nf to work properly, you must use the shell command "set" to set the
environment variable "term" to the correct name, above.  For instance,

	% set term=heath

if you are using a z19 or a heathkit terminal.  If an unknown type of
terminal is specified, you will end up in "vi".  To escape from "vi",
type two capital Z's, and you should be all right.

Implementation notes:

	The editor is implemented using the following scheme:

	The editing buffer is divided into two parts, refered to as the
	left stack and the right stack.  The left stack contains characters
	to the left of the cursor, and similarly for the right stack.
	The top of one of the stacks (I forget which one) is the current
	character.

	Using this technique, insertions and deletions are done by
	quite local manipulations of either stack, and movement in
	the file is done by pushing and popping the stacks in parallel.

	This makes file movement somewhat more expensive than insertions and
	deletions, but some optimizations have been made (the stacks are
	not actually pushed and popped in parallel).  Fortunately, the
	Vax is so fast this is of little trouble.

